WEBVTT

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:36.750
<v Ira Glass>Hey there, everybody. Ira here. So if you heard our show last week, you know that I had just learned hours before we broadcast that episode that I needed to go into quarantine because a guy who I had shaken hands with five days before that developed symptoms and then tested positive for coronavirus. It is now 12 days since I shook hands with that guy, and I've not developed symptoms. CDC guidelines say that it can happen for up to 14 days, so I am nearly in the clear. A reminder to everybody that people who don't have symptoms can still be carrying the virus and give it to others. Anyway, the world has changed so much, right?
<v Ira Glass>大家好，我是艾拉。如果你们听了我们上周的节目，就知道我在那期节目播出前几个小时才刚得知，我需要进行隔离，因为我在五天前曾与一个人握手，那个人随后出现了症状，新冠病毒检测结果呈阳性。从我与那个人握手到现在已经过去了12天，我还没有出现症状。美国疾控中心（CDC）的指导方针称症状可能会在14天内出现，所以我差不多算脱离危险了。提醒大家，没有症状的人仍可能携带病毒并传染给他人。总之，世界已经发生了巨大的变化，对吧？

00:00:36.750 --> 00:01:01.800
<v Ira Glass>One of the striking things about this coronavirus is how differently it affects different people. So some people who get it have no symptoms, or they get mild symptoms, or they experience something like a bad flu. That's particularly young and healthy people, though young, healthy adults are not immune. The CDC put out a report this week-- maybe you saw this-- saying 20% of hospitalized COVID-19 patients are between 20 and 44 years old.
<v Ira Glass>这种冠状病毒最引人注目的一点是它对不同人的影响差异很大。有些人感染后完全没有症状，或者症状轻微，亦或是经历了像重感冒一样的过程。对于年轻人和健康人尤其如此，尽管年轻、健康的成年人也并非免疫。美国疾控中心本周发布了一份报告——或许你们看到了——称在住院的新冠肺炎患者中，20%的人年龄在20岁到44岁之间。

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:32.150
<v Ira Glass>But it's older people, as we all know, who are living in a completely different world these days. If they leave their homes, they're walking around in a world that's suddenly filled with this invisible thing that can kill them with no idea of who around them might have it or what doorknob it might be on. One of our producers, David Kestenbaum, talked to this woman, Tova Rothman, who's 71. Her husband's 74 and has a compromised immune system, so he is in special danger if he comes into contact with the virus. Here's David.
<v Ira Glass>但我们都知道，对于老年人来说，他们如今生活在一个完全不同的世界里。如果他们走出家门，他们就是在这样一个世界里走动：这个世界突然充斥着这种肉眼看不见、却能夺走他们生命的东西，他们根本不知道身边的人谁可能携带病毒，也不知道病毒可能附着在哪个门把手上。我们的一位制片人戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆采访了71岁的托娃·罗斯曼。她丈夫今年74岁，免疫系统受损，因此如果接触到病毒，他将面临特殊的危险。下面请听戴维的报道。

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:48.740
<v David Kestenbaum>I talked to Tova last weekend, which feels like so long ago. I don't think I would do this now, but we met in person to talk. She lives in my town, in this apartment across from the pizza place, the little movie theater, the Kings supermarket. Her daughter told me she was eager to talk to someone.
<v David Kestenbaum>上周末我和托娃通了话，那感觉像是很久以前的事了。我想现在我不会再这么做了，但当时我们面对面进行了交谈。她住在我所在的城镇，公寓就在披萨店、小电影院和金斯超市对面。她女儿告诉我，她很渴望和人说话。

00:01:48.740 --> 00:02:03.580
<v David Kestenbaum>For her safety, I checked my temperature before I left the house, washed my hands twice, and wiped down my microphone, though even that is no guarantee. Again, I wouldn't do this now. She came down from her apartment carrying her cell phone and a canister of Clorox wipes.
<v David Kestenbaum>为了她的安全，我离家前量了体温，洗了两次手，并擦拭了我的麦克风，尽管即便如此也无法保证绝对安全。再说一次，换做现在我不会这么做了。她带着手机和一罐高乐氏消毒湿巾从公寓走下来。

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.140
<v Tova Rothman>Oh, you brought your own wipes too.
<v Tova Rothman>哦，你也自带了湿巾。

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:05.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:21.680
<v Ira Glass>We did the interview on a park bench. If she's a little off-mic sometimes, it's because I'm sitting as far away from her as possible, holding my arms straight out with the microphone. Every move she makes right now requires some impossible calculation. She has to avoid anything that might get her or her husband sick.
<v Ira Glass>我们在公园长椅上进行了采访。如果她有时声音听着不太清楚，那是因为我尽量坐得离她远远的，手臂伸得笔直拿着麦克风。她现在做的每一个动作都需要经过近乎不可能的精确计算。她必须避开任何可能让她或她丈夫生病的东西。

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.910
<v David Kestenbaum>How are you feeling in general?
<v David Kestenbaum>你总体感觉怎么样？

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:47.780
<v Tova Rothman>Overwhelmed, but I feel like that I can master this. I went to Kings across the street. I just try to stay away from people. They have them restocking and restocking. And they actually have better fruit and fresher fruit now. [LAUGHS]
<v Tova Rothman>不知所措，但我感觉自己能够掌控这一切。我去过街对面的金斯超市。我只是尽量远离人群。他们一直在补货。而且他们现在的水果品质更好，也更新鲜了。[笑声]

00:02:47.780 --> 00:03:10.250
<v Tova Rothman>But you're not supposed to get between six feet of people. And millennials are all marching around buying things, and you're, whoa, don't come near me. I must look like I'm crazy, but that's the way it goes.
<v Tova Rothman>但是你不应该在距离他人六英尺（约1.8米）以内的范围内。那些千禧一代到处走动买东西，而你心里想的是，哇，别靠近我。我看起来一定像个疯子，但情况就是这样。

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:23.780
<v Tova Rothman>This town is a very young town, and so there's a lot of young people and a lot of kids skateboarding. And they're in your face. And you're like, stay away. But they don't understand that.
<v Tova Rothman>这个镇是个非常年轻的镇，有很多年轻人和玩滑板的孩子。他们就在你眼前晃悠。而你的反应是，离远点。但他们不理解。

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.870
<v David Kestenbaum>Have you said anything to anybody?
<v David Kestenbaum>你有没有跟谁说过什么？

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:38.930
<v Tova Rothman>No. Wearing my plastic gloves and carrying my canister of Clorox, I think they understand where I'm coming from. [LAUGHS] It sounds so funny.
<v Tova Rothman>没有。戴着橡胶手套，拎着我的高乐氏消毒湿巾，我想他们明白我的立场。[笑声] 这听起来太滑稽了。

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.740
<v David Kestenbaum>Her husband, because of his immune system, is stuck in the apartment.
<v David Kestenbaum>她的丈夫因为免疫系统的问题，被困在公寓里。

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:49.680
<v Tova Rothman>My husband is not well. He can't go out, and he's not happy about it.
<v Tova Rothman>我丈夫身体不好。他不能出去，他对此很不高兴。

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:59.420
<v David Kestenbaum>There's TV, of course, but annoyingly, no sports to watch because everything's been canceled. The other night, he had Gunsmoke on, which is really not Tova's thing.
<v David Kestenbaum>当然有电视看，但烦人的是，因为一切都取消了，没有体育节目可看。前几天晚上，他在看《荒野大镖客》，那可不是托娃喜欢的节目。

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:14.870
<v Tova Rothman>So we're not having a great relationship with this. I told him that I was checking on him, and he shouldn't get out of his chair. And he very nastily said, OK, I won't get out.
<v Tova Rothman>所以我们在这方面的关系不太好。我告诉他我要去看看他，他不应该从椅子上站起来。他非常刻薄地说，好吧，我不站起来。

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:17.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Does he say, I want to go outside, and you say, we can't?
<v David Kestenbaum>他会说“我想出去”，而你说“我们不能出去”吗？

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:41.330
<v Tova Rothman>Yeah. He'll say, I want to do something. And I say, you can't. I would take him downstairs, and he could go out and get air in his face. But there's so many surfaces between our apartment and the lobby, and it's only one floor. But there's so much you have to worry about.
<v Tova Rothman>是的。他会说，我想做点什么。我说，你不能做。我本想带他下楼，让他出去吹吹风。但我们的公寓到大厅之间有太多表面要接触，虽然只有一层楼。但有太多东西需要担心了。

00:04:41.330 --> 00:05:01.500
<v Tova Rothman>It is a surreal thing. You just-- you think, what? Every morning, you wake up and think, I can't believe this. Is it real? So, it's real.
<v Tova Rothman>这真是件超现实的事情。你只是——你会想，什么？每天早上醒来，你都会想，我真不敢相信。这是真的吗？所以，这是真的。

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.280
<v David Kestenbaum>Tova says her grandfather died from the Spanish flu in 1918.
<v David Kestenbaum>托娃说她的祖父在1918年的西班牙流感中去世了。

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:22.780
<v Tova Rothman>He went to the opera. They used to get tickets where they could stand and pay a lot less. And so he went to the opera, and he was in a crowd. And he got-- he was sick one day and died the next.
<v Tova Rothman>他去看了歌剧。他们过去常买那种可以站着观看、票价便宜得多的票。所以他去了歌剧院，身处人群之中。他——他有一天病了，第二天就去世了。

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:33.140
<v David Kestenbaum>He was 26. I keep thinking about how differently everyone might view this thing, how different our country's response might have been if the virus were as dangerous for younger people.
<v David Kestenbaum>他才26岁。我一直在想，如果病毒对年轻人同样危险，大家对这件事的看法会有多大不同，我们国家的应对措施又会如何不同。

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:54.620
<v Tova Rothman>Maybe we would have reacted faster because, oh, it's only the old people. [LAUGHS] That's how I feel. It wasn't quite as tragic if it's the old people. My friends keep calling and saying, who knew we were the old people, which is true.
<v Tova Rothman>也许我们会反应得更快，因为，哦，那只是老年人。[笑声] 我就是这么觉得的。如果是老年人，似乎就不那么悲剧了。我的朋友们一直在打电话说，谁知道我们竟然就是那个“老年人”群体，这确实是真的。

00:05:54.620 --> 00:06:05.060
<v David Kestenbaum>Tova stood up to go back to her husband. And as she did, this guy walked really close to her to throw a tissue in the trash can, as if it was two weeks ago or something. Tova was not happy.
<v David Kestenbaum>托娃站起身准备回去找她丈夫。就在这时，一个男人从她身边走得非常近，去往垃圾桶里扔纸巾，就好像现在还是两周前或者什么类似的时候。托娃很不高兴。

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:15.200
<v Tova Rothman>I was almost ready to throw my canister of wipes at him. He should stay away. Maybe I don't look 71. [LAUGHS]
<v Tova Rothman>我当时差点想把我的消毒湿巾罐扔向他。他应该保持距离。也许我看起来不像71岁。[笑声]

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:15.960
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm sure that's it.
<v David Kestenbaum>我肯定是因为这个。

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.340
<v Tova Rothman>Ah, yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v Tova Rothman>啊，是啊。[笑声]

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:18.810
<v David Kestenbaum>OK.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的。

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.810
<v Tova Rothman>Oh, very nice meeting you too.
<v Tova Rothman>哦，见到你也很高兴。

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:21.230
<v David Kestenbaum>OK.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的。

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.840
<v Tova Rothman>And thank you.
<v Tova Rothman>谢谢你。

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:36.220
<v David Kestenbaum>I called Tova four days later to see how she was doing. She's still going out for walks-- no supermarkets anymore.
<v David Kestenbaum>四天后我打电话给托娃，看看她过得怎么样。她仍然会出去散步——只是不再去超市了。

00:06:36.220 --> 00:07:02.450
<v Ira Glass>That was actually the very last interview that anybody on our staff did with somebody in person, David Kestenbaum's story. We have all been holed up in our own homes, everybody on the staff. We are one of the many businesses that have decided that it's safer for everybody if we're working from home. So everybody's doing interviews over the phone. We're all preparing stories for next week's show about what's happening in the country and in the world.
<v Ira Glass>那实际上是我们的员工采访的最后一个人，也就是戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆的那个故事。我们所有人都窝在自己的家里，员工中的每一个人。我们是许多决定居家办公对大家更安全的企业之一。所以每个人都在通过电话进行采访。我们都在为下周的节目准备关于国家和世界正在发生什么的故事。

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:55.770
<v Ira Glass>But in the meantime, until then, thinking about what might be nice to hear on the radio in a moment like this one. I think so much of what is happening now, as we're being told to stay home, and so many of us are either cooped up with our families or reaching out to family members more than usual, and reaching out to the other people we love to see how they're doing, and to worry together about what's going to happen next, we thought, with that in mind, today we would prepare a show of stories that we've made over the years about families and about people trying to find comfort or find answers by turning to their families, including-- we have this one story from 25 years ago of me and my mom back when she was still alive. And I saw her quoted in a national magazine as a sexpert. I had some questions for her, and I called her. We get to that in Act Three.
<v Ira Glass>但在那之前，考虑到在这样一个时刻，收音机里播放什么内容会比较好。我想，现在所发生的事情中，很大一部分是当大家被告知待在家里时，我们很多人要么是被关在家里和家人在一起，要么比平时更多地联系家人，联系我们所爱的人，看看他们过得怎么样，并一起担忧接下来会发生什么。考虑到这一点，我们认为今天应该准备一期节目，分享我们多年来制作的关于家庭的故事，以及人们试图通过转向家人来寻找安慰或答案的故事，包括——我们有这个25年前的故事，关于我和我妈妈，那时候她还活着。我看到她在一本全国性的杂志上被引用为“性专家”。我有一些问题想问她，于是我打了电话给她。我们将在第三幕中探讨。

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:10.690
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:30.690
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Hey, Dad. Dad!" So school was cut off in so many places, and kids are home. And we have this story of a parent trying to go about his day and his daughter coming to him over and over with some things on her mind. Stephanie Foo tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“嘿，爸爸。爸爸！”因为在许多地方学校停课了，孩子们都待在家里。我们有一个故事，讲的是一位父亲试图继续他的一天，而他的女儿一次又一次地带着心中的疑问来找他。斯蒂芬妮·傅讲述了发生的事情。

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:39.540
<v Stephanie Foo>My friend Matt's older daughter went through one of those hardcore phases, where she got really into asking her dad a lot of questions. She was nine.
<v Stephanie Foo>我朋友马特的大女儿经历了一个“硬核”阶段，那时她非常喜欢问爸爸很多问题。她当时九岁。

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:57.450
<v Matt>There's the why phase, and then the why phase can turn into the why not and explain, and that endless string of questions, like, why can't I have my own room? How do I get to school? Why can't we have a yard? Can I have a cookie? They're unrelenting.
<v Matt>有“为什么”阶段，然后“为什么”阶段可能会变成“为什么不”以及“解释一下”，还有没完没了的问题串，比如，为什么我不能有自己的房间？我怎么去学校？为什么我们没有院子？我可以吃块饼干吗？这些问题接连不断。

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:02.250
<v Stephanie Foo>So one night, Matt was working from home, and Rosie was bugging him with her questions.
<v Stephanie Foo>所以有一天晚上，马特在家里工作，罗茜一直在用她的问题打扰他。

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:18.600
<v Matt>And it was just sort of one after the other after the other. And I was like, all right, look, you got to just give me a minute. I'm working right now. Just go off and write them all down. Make me a list of the questions that you want me to answer, and I'll answer them for you.
<v Matt>就是一个接一个。我就说，好吧，听着，你得给我一点时间。我正在工作。你去把它们都写下来。列一张你想让我回答的问题清单，我会为你回答的。

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:34.370
<v Matt>I thought it was going to be like three or four questions and then a picture of a rabbit or something. And I get this list, and I look at it. And these are the essential, unanswerable questions of life.
<v Matt>我以为那会是三四个问题，然后画个兔子什么的。结果我拿到了这张清单，看了一眼。这些都是关于生命的基本的、无法回答的问题。

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:37.950
<v Stephanie Foo>Read a few of these questions for me. Start at the very top.
<v Stephanie Foo>帮我读几个问题。从最上面开始。

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.760
<v Matt>OK. So, what is life? Why?
<v Matt>好的。那么，生命是什么？为什么？

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:41.590
<v Stephanie Foo>That's the first question?
<v Stephanie Foo>那是第一个问题？

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:43.610
<v Matt>That's the first question. It's the first thing she wants to know.
<v Matt>那是第一个问题。这是她想知道的第一件事。

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.220
<v Matt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Matt>[笑声]

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:55.930
<v Matt>Where do we go when we die-- heaven? Explain. Another planet? Is heaven another planet? Why is there heaven or hell? Time-- why? Explain.
<v Matt>我们死后去哪里——天堂吗？解释一下。另一个星球？天堂是另一个星球吗？为什么会有天堂或地狱？时间——为什么？解释一下。

00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:00.900
<v Stephanie Foo>[LAUGHS]
<v Stephanie Foo>[笑声]

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:11.280
<v Matt>Do we make worlds? Do we become like God-- why? Why do you do what you do? How do you know what's true? Who do you miss-- why? Explain.
<v Matt>我们创造世界吗？我们变得像上帝一样吗——为什么？你为什么做你现在所做的事？你怎么知道什么是真的？你思念谁——为什么？解释一下。

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:39.490
<v Matt>Do you miss anyone more than them? And does that change, and how? And if that changes, was it worth missing them in the first place? And my favorite is-- pretty much just my jaw dropped-- why any of this? I mean, my first reaction to them is-- I mean, I'm proud of her. And then I realize I actually have to answer these questions.
<v Matt>你是否比他们更想念任何人？这种情况会改变吗，如何改变？如果改变了，最初思念他们值得吗？我最喜欢的是——我简直惊掉了下巴——为什么会有这一切？我的第一反应是——我是说，我为她感到骄傲。然后我意识到我真的必须回答这些问题。

00:10:39.490 --> 00:11:03.490
<v Stephanie Foo>There are about three pages, single-spaced, of handwritten questions, about 50 questions total. But a promise was a promise, so Matt got to work. He's a professor at West Point, teaches writing. And so he took a professorial approach to it and started researching answers for her, looking up quotes on each topic, spending weeks, sometimes months, writing each answer.
<v Stephanie Foo>总共有大约三页单倍行距的手写问题，总共大约50个问题。但承诺就是承诺，所以马特开始工作了。他在西点军校当教授，教写作。于是他采取了教授式的方法，开始为她研究答案，查找每个主题的引语，花了数周，有时甚至是数月，来写每一个答案。

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:07.370
<v Stephanie Foo>What's the shortest and what's the longest you've ever spent, and what's the hardest one?
<v Stephanie Foo>你花过最短和最长的时间分别是什么，最难的问题是哪一个？

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:12.940
<v Matt>So I think the longest one is one that I haven't finished answering for her yet, which is, what is love?
<v Matt>我想最长的一个是我还没有完成回答的，那就是，什么是爱？

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:16.210
<v Stephanie Foo>What's been the easiest one to answer so far?
<v Stephanie Foo>到目前为止最好回答的是哪一个？

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:24.910
<v Matt>Is heaven another planet? No.
<v Matt>天堂是另一个星球吗？不是。

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:31.030
<v Stephanie Foo>I got him to read me one of the answers he worked hardest on. The answer to time-- why? Explain.
<v Stephanie Foo>我让他读给我听其中一个他花了最大心血准备的答案。关于“时间——为什么？解释一下”的答案。

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:32.750
<v Stephanie Foo>Could you read it for me?
<v Stephanie Foo>你能读给我听吗？

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:50.360
<v Matt>Sure. So tell me what and tell me why, and the burden is on me to justify this to you. Perhaps that's what time means in the end, is justification or a lack of being justified. And I don't really know what justification means. There was an old movie I saw when I was a kid in your grandmother's house--
<v Matt>当然。所以告诉我“什么”和“为什么”，这重担就落在我的肩上，要向你证明这一点。也许这就是时间最终的含义，是证明或者缺乏被证明。我并不真正知道“证明”意味着什么。我小时候在你祖母家看过一部老电影——

00:11:50.360 --> 00:12:00.000
<v Stephanie Foo>He quotes Camus, then brings in the Millennium Falcon, then St. Augustine, then Kierkegaard. Rosie was nine. All his answers are like this.
<v Stephanie Foo>他引用了加缪的话，然后引入了千年隼号，接着是圣奥古斯丁，然后是基尔克果。罗茜当时九岁。他所有的回答都像这样。

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.070
<v Matt>Kierkegaard gets to this point after either/or'ing everything. He says, why did I not die as a baby?
<v Matt>基尔克果在把一切“非此即彼”之后得出了这个结论。他说，我为什么没有作为一个婴儿死去？

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:17.640
<v Stephanie Foo>I'm a grown-up, and I find it impossible to follow your answers. Honestly, I have not any idea what you're saying.
<v Stephanie Foo>我是个成年人，我觉得你的回答完全无法理解。老实说，我根本不知道你在说什么。

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:27.010
<v Matt>OK. [LAUGHS] Yeah. I mean, I really don't understand half of what I just said either, to be honest.
<v Matt>好吧。[笑声] 是的。说实话，我真的连自己刚才说的一半内容都不明白。

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:32.410
<v Stephanie Foo>What his answers do have going for them is sincerity. The time one ends like this.
<v Stephanie Foo>他的答案确实有真诚的地方。关于时间的那个答案是这样结尾的。

00:12:32.410 --> 00:13:04.330
<v Matt>In one of my favorite stories by a guy named William Faulkner, there's a daddy who gives his kid a watch and says, I give you this watch, not that you might remember time, but so that you might forget it for a little while. I can only tell you that time is me turning and turning while the world is turning around a star that turns around a center that turns around the whole time. Among all the other things, the little turning animals on all the little turning worlds, there is me, trying to turn to you.
<v Matt>在威廉·福克纳写的一个我最喜欢的故事里，有一位父亲给孩子一块手表，说，我给你这块手表，不是为了让你记住时间，而是为了让你在短时间内忘记它。我只能告诉你，时间是我在旋转，同时世界围绕着一颗恒星旋转，那颗恒星围绕着一个中心旋转，整个时间都在旋转。在所有事物中，在所有转动的小世界上的转动小动物中，有我，试图转动向你。

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:09.210
<v Stephanie Foo>OK. And you just told her this answer like this?
<v Stephanie Foo>好吧。你就这样把这个答案告诉她了吗？

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:09.840
<v Matt>Yeah.
<v Matt>是的。

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.370
<v Stephanie Foo>And she--
<v Stephanie Foo>然后她——

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:25.200
<v Matt>I mean, she would kind of pass in and out of being interested in it. And at the end of it she's just kind of like, oh, yeah, OK. And I'm like, all right, well.
<v Matt>我是说，她有时会对它感兴趣，有时又不感兴趣。最后她只是那种，哦，是的，好的。我就说，好吧，好吧。

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:37.310
<v Matt>I mean, do you see what I'm saying about time? It's a measurement of change. It's an arbitrary human construct, but not, but it feels different, so it's phenomena. She's like, yeah, yeah, OK.
<v Matt>我是说，你明白我关于时间的说法吗？它是一种对变化的衡量。它是一个任意的人类建构，但又不完全是，因为它感觉不同，所以它是一种现象。她就说，是的，是的，好的。

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:41.100
<v Rosie>I was like, oh, well, this isn't really exactly what I wanted.
<v Rosie>我就觉得，哦，好吧，这并不完全是我想要的。

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.230
<v Stephanie Foo>That's not what you wanted because you were like, oh, this is kind of boring?
<v Stephanie Foo>这不是你想要的，因为你当时想的是，哦，这有点无聊？

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.410
<v Rosie>Yeah.
<v Rosie>是的。

00:13:45.410 --> 00:14:05.660
<v Stephanie Foo>Rosie has a pixie cut and a cheeky grin. She gave her dad the 50 questions three years ago. She's 12 now. He's been working on getting her answers, but he's only gone through 2/3 of them because it takes him so long to cobble together a response. What I found out talking to Rosie is she didn't even really care about the answers to these questions.
<v Stephanie Foo>罗茜剪着精灵短发，带着淘气的笑容。她三年前给了她爸爸这50个问题。她现在12岁了。他一直在努力回答她，但他只完成了三分之二，因为他花太长时间拼凑出一个回应了。我和罗茜交谈后发现，她甚至并不真正在乎这些问题的答案。

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:12.520
<v Rosie>Questions that I thought that would take him a long time to answer, because at the time I really just wanted to talk to him.
<v Rosie>我以为他会花很长时间回答的问题，因为当时我真的只是想跟他说话。

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:35.470
<v Stephanie Foo>It all started when she first moved to New York City. Before then, she'd been living with her mom and grandparents most of the week. But then her grandpa, who she was really close to, died, and she had to move in with her dad during the week instead. At the same time, she started at a new school where the kids either ignored or bullied her, and she felt lost. One day, she came home from school and decided she needed to do something about it.
<v Stephanie Foo>这一切始于她第一次搬到纽约市时。在此之前，她大部分时间都和妈妈及祖父母住在一起。但后来她非常亲近的祖父去世了，她不得不工作日搬去和爸爸住。与此同时，她进了一所新学校，那里的孩子要么无视她，要么欺负她，她感到很迷茫。有一天，她放学回家，决定做点什么。

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:52.660
<v Rosie>I was lonely, and I felt a little sad that nobody had really stepped out to say, oh, hey, it's going to be OK. I'll be your friend. So that's when I really, really needed somebody to talk to.
<v Rosie>我很孤独，我感到有点难过，因为没有人真正站出来说，哦，嘿，一切都会好起来的。我会成为你的朋友。所以那时我真的、真的需要有人说话。

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:55.030
<v Stephanie Foo>So you didn't have anybody to talk to at school?
<v Stephanie Foo>所以你在学校没有人说话？

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:55.810
<v Rosie>No.
<v Rosie>没有。

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:57.600
<v Stephanie Foo>And then at home?
<v Stephanie Foo>那在家里呢？

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:10.810
<v Rosie>No. That's really why I felt like, oh, this is my dad. He's a really important person. I love him very much. I really want to become closer with him. I wish there was something that I could do to make us closer.
<v Rosie>没有。这就是为什么我真的觉得，哦，这是我的爸爸。他是一个很重要的人。我非常爱他。我真的想和他变得更亲近。我希望有什么事情能让我们变得更亲近。

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:13.730
<v Stephanie Foo>Did you feel like your dad wasn't paying enough attention to you?
<v Stephanie Foo>你觉得你爸爸没怎么注意你吗？

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:18.280
<v Rosie>Yeah, a little bit. Or not a little bit, yeah.
<v Rosie>是的，有一点。或者说不只是有一点，是的。

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:20.860
<v Stephanie Foo>What was he doing instead?
<v Stephanie Foo>他当时在做什么？

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:59.450
<v Rosie>He was writing papers on his computer. And I knew, at the time, how important it was, but part of me still wished that, like, put down all the screens, put down everything else, and just talk. So I wrote all the questions down, and they were big questions because I know my dad. And if it's a little question, he'll elaborate on it, and he'll make it a big deal. So if you times that by a big complex question, that would be a huge talk.
<v Rosie>他在电脑上写论文。我当时知道那有多重要，但我心里还是希望，放下所有的屏幕，放下其他一切，只是聊聊天。所以我把所有问题都写了下来，而且它们都是大问题，因为我了解我爸爸。如果是一个小问题，他会详细阐述，把它当成一件大事。所以如果你把它乘以一个复杂的大问题，那会是一次巨大的谈话。

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:02.330
<v Stephanie Foo>Is it true that you weren't talking to her much at the time?
<v Stephanie Foo>当时你是不是没怎么跟她说话？

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:17.450
<v Matt>No, I think I was talking to her all the time. I would tell her it's time to get up and go to school. I would tell her that it was time to do her homework. I would tell her that she needed a new jacket. Yeah, I mean, I talked to you all the time.
<v Matt>没有，我觉得我一直在跟她说话。我会告诉她该起床去上学了。我会告诉她该做作业了。我会告诉她需要一件新外套。是的，我是说，我一直都在跟你说话。

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:22.750
<v Stephanie Foo>Maybe you're noticing the purely logistical nature of everything he mentioned. It certainly didn't get past Rosie.
<v Stephanie Foo>也许你注意到了他提到的所有事情纯粹是后勤性的。罗茜肯定没漏掉这一点。

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.020
<v Matt>I talked to you all the time.
<v Matt>我一直在跟你说话。

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:48.170
<v Rosie>Yeah, but to me, it's not really the same thing. So conversation and talking are completely different things. Talking could be a range from, oh, hey, what's up? And conversation is you're deep in thought, and you're looking, and you're making eye contact, and you're really enjoying the presence of somebody else.
<v Rosie>是的，但对我来说，那并不完全是一回事。谈话和说话是完全不同的事情。说话的范围可以是，哦，嘿，怎么了？而谈话是你沉思其中，你在观察，你在进行眼神交流，你真的在享受另一个人的存在。

00:16:48.170 --> 00:17:21.150
<v Stephanie Foo>Rosie's a smart kid, yeah, but this is the thing I really admire about her. Matt was a single dad with two kids, going to school and trying to make the rent at the same time. Telling him to pay attention to her didn't cut it, so she figured out something else. I read this short story recently about a successful con man whose motto was, make them want to give you the thing you want to take. Rosie made her dad want to give her attention by making an opportunity to do what he loved, ponder over life's big questions.
<v Stephanie Foo>罗茜是个聪明的孩子，是的，但这是我真正钦佩她的地方。马特是一个单身爸爸，带着两个孩子，又要上学又要付房租。告诉他多关注她并没有起到作用，所以她想出了别的办法。我最近读过一个短篇小说，讲的是一个成功的骗子，他的座右铭是：让他们想要给你你想拿的东西。罗茜通过创造机会让他做他热爱的事情——思考人生的大问题，从而让爸爸想要给她关注。

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:39.240
<v Rosie>My dad was kind of hard-shelled, I guess. And so it took a lot. I had to keep asking these questions and keep wondering. For me, it was just-- I had to keep going, and keep trying, and keep being this little bird that goes on your shoulders, like, I'm now your friend.
<v Rosie>我爸爸有点外冷内热。所以这花了很多功夫。我不得不一直问这些问题，一直好奇。对我来说，这只是——我必须坚持下去，一直尝试，一直像这只落在你肩膀上的小鸟一样，比如，我现在是你的朋友了。

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:43.440
<v Stephanie Foo>Do you feel like it sort of taught him how to talk to you better?
<v Stephanie Foo>你觉得这是否教了他如何更好地跟你说话？

00:17:43.440 --> 00:18:00.740
<v Rosie>Yeah, definitely. Over the past three years, we've really worked on having actual conversations than him just answering questions for me because we practice it.
<v Rosie>是的，绝对是。在过去三年里，我们真正致力于进行实际的对话，而不是他只是回答我的问题，因为我们练习过了。

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:12.120
<v Stephanie Foo>Rosie never knew that her dad spent months and months writing down each answer. Matt only told her when I started working on this story. And she said she felt like, what? Are you kidding me?
<v Stephanie Foo>罗茜从来不知道她爸爸花了几个月又几个月的时间写下每个答案。马特只有在我开始制作这个故事时才告诉她。她说她觉得，什么？你在开玩笑吗？

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:23.500
<v Rosie>I had no idea that he was doing all these things, and it was just a big surprise for me. If I could, I would definitely just say forget the questions. I just want to talk.
<v Rosie>我根本不知道他在做所有这些事情，这对我来说只是一个巨大的惊喜。如果可以，我绝对会说忘了那些问题吧。我只是想聊聊天。

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.920
<v Stephanie Foo>So you're like, well, you don't even have to go through all that trouble.
<v Stephanie Foo>所以你就说，好吧，你甚至不用费那么大劲。

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:27.790
<v Rosie>Yeah.
<v Rosie>是的。

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:28.630
<v Stephanie Foo>Just hang out.
<v Stephanie Foo>只是待在一起。

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:30.270
<v Rosie>Yeah.
<v Rosie>是的。

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:34.350
<v Stephanie Foo>Rosie said this to her dad when she found out, and it really threw him for a loop.
<v Stephanie Foo>罗茜发现后对她爸爸说了这些，这真的让他不知所措。

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:37.750
<v Matt>Yeah, it was a complete waste of time. [LAUGHS]
<v Matt>是的，这完全是浪费时间。[笑声]

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:49.730
<v Matt>I mean, what a complete waste of time to come up with these big, extensive projects that-- they were definitely less important to them than just listening to them.
<v Matt>我是说，想出这些大型的、广泛的项目来，这真是完全浪费时间——相比于倾听他们，这些对他们来说肯定不那么重要。

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:51.480
<v Stephanie Foo>Hm.
<v Stephanie Foo>嗯。

00:18:51.480 --> 00:19:13.770
<v Matt>What is time-- why? Explain. Well, I can tell you what I don't want time to be. I don't want time to be something where I am just figuring out that I need to shut up and make some time to listen to this little kid before it's too late.
<v Matt>时间是什么——为什么？解释一下。嗯，我可以告诉你我不想要时间成为什么。我不想要时间成为那种我只是在发现我需要闭嘴并抽出一些时间来倾听这个小孩的地方，在一切为时已晚之前。

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:35.160
<v Stephanie Foo>Rosie really started asking the questions because she wanted to know that she wasn't alone and that everything was going to be OK. Now she enjoys hearing the answers because they remind her that that's true. That's why one of her favorite answers is the ending of time-- why? Explain-- the part about all of the planets turning around themselves and in the middle of it Matt turning towards Rosie.
<v Stephanie Foo>罗茜问这些问题真的是因为她想知道她并不孤单，一切都会好起来。现在她很喜欢听这些答案，因为它们提醒她这是真的。这就是为什么她最喜欢的答案之一是关于“时间——为什么？解释一下”的结尾——关于所有行星围绕自身转动，而在这一切中间马特转向罗茜的部分。

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:56.110
<v Rosie>One of the meanings of that is even though everything is happening around you, he just wants to know about me a little more, I guess. And a thing that I really like about that is because he just uses these sentences that make me happy when I read them.
<v Rosie>其中的一个意思是，即使周围发生的一切都在发生，他只是想多了解我一点，我想。我真正喜欢它的一点是因为他只是使用了这些我在读的时候让我感到快乐的句子。

00:19:56.110 --> 00:20:00.360
<v Stephanie Foo>It's kind of funny. When he read this to me, he sort of choked up a little bit.
<v Stephanie Foo>挺有趣的。当他读这些给我听的时候，他有点哽咽。

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:18.960
<v Rosie>Yeah. He likes to be kind of a one-expression person, but when he reads stuff like this he gets all emotional. In the car ride here, he was like, oh, I love you so much. And he was tearing up and looking out the window, so.
<v Rosie>是的。他喜欢表现得像是一个只有一种表情的人，但当他读这样的东西时，他会变得很感性。在开车的路上，他会说，哦，我太爱你了。他会泪流满面，看向窗外，所以。

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:23.310
<v Stephanie Foo>[LAUGHS] You look so happy about that.
<v Stephanie Foo>[笑声] 你看起来为此感到很高兴。

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.160
<v Rosie>Yeah, it's pretty great.
<v Rosie>是的，这很棒。

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:34.570
<v Rosie>[LAUGHTER]
<v Rosie>[笑声]

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:51.400
<v Stephanie Foo>So people have been asking these big, important questions like, why are we here? What is life-- forever? And do you think that the real big reason why we ask it is to have a reason to talk to each other?
<v Stephanie Foo>所以人们一直在问这些重大的、重要的问题，比如，我们为什么在这里？生命是什么——永远吗？你认为我们问这些问题的真正重大原因是有一个理由互相交谈吗？

00:20:51.400 --> 00:21:09.010
<v Rosie>No. I think that philosophers actually really do wonder about these things, and they don't use it so that they can talk to their dads more. They use it because they really wonder about these things, and they want to know everything about it. But for my personal use, yes. That's exactly it.
<v Rosie>不。我认为哲学家们确实真的很想知道这些事情，他们使用它不是为了让他们能更多地跟爸爸说话。他们使用它是因为他们真的想知道这些事情，他们想了解关于它的一切。但就我个人而言，是的。完全是这样。

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:28.010
<v Stephanie Foo>Matt does still want to keep answering Rosie's questions for her, but as for the hardest question, what is love, I don't think Rosie needs her dad to explain that to her anymore. She gets it.
<v Stephanie Foo>马特确实仍然想继续回答罗茜的问题，但至于那个最难的问题，“什么是爱”，我想罗茜不需要她爸爸再向她解释了。她懂了。

00:21:28.010 --> 00:22:02.270
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo. She's currently writing a book about complex PTSD. These days, Rosie is a freshman in high school, and she goes by Rory now. We checked in with her. She says she's using her time cooped up at home with her family right now to draw and listen to music. She says everybody is getting along.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·傅。她目前正在写一本关于复杂创伤后应激障碍（CPTSD）的书。如今，罗茜是一名高中新生，她现在叫罗里。我们跟她确认过。她说她现在正在利用被关在家里与家人在一起的时间来画画和听音乐。她说大家相处得很好。

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:18.170
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "Call Me Maybe." So many of us are concerned right now about our older relatives, especially our parents and grandparents. And this story, in the most literal way, is about reaching out over the phone. Sean Cole put it together.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“也许给我打个电话”。我们很多人现在都在关心我们的年长亲属，特别是我们的父母和祖父母。而这个故事，以最字面的方式，讲述了通过电话联系。肖恩·科尔整理了这个故事。

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:20.960
<v Ira Glass>[DIAL TONE]
<v Ira Glass>[拨号音]

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:25.130
<v Sean's Mom>Yo, Sean. This is your mom. I wasn't home.
<v Sean's Mom>喂，肖恩。我是你妈。我刚才不在家。

00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:47.000
<v Sean Cole>About four or five years ago, I started saving all my mom's voicemails, thinking, she's not going to be around forever. I'm going to want to hear her voice when she's gone, though somehow I didn't extend that thought to I could hear her voice right now if I picked up the phone. She had a talent for the form. With some of them, it almost feels like I'm talking to her, except she's playing both parts.
<v Sean Cole>大约四五年前，我开始保存我妈妈所有的语音留言，我想，她不会永远都在。等她走了，我会想听听她的声音，虽然我不知何故没有想到，如果我拿起电话，我现在就能听到她的声音。她在这一方面很有天赋。听着其中的一些，感觉几乎就像我在跟她对话，只不过她分饰了两角。

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:55.130
<v Sean's Mom>What time is it? Oh, it's 6:52, huh? Yeah, it's later than I thought. I can't believe it's still light out.
<v Sean's Mom>几点了？哦，6点52分，是吗？是的，比我想象的要晚。真不敢相信天还亮着。

00:22:55.130 --> 00:23:15.100
<v Sean's Mom>Decided to go to the Royal for supper tonight, so that's what we did. We went early, and we are back early. So I don't know what you're doing. So this is me. C'est moi. I'm here. Right. Yo aqui.
<v Sean's Mom>决定今晚去皇家餐厅吃晚饭，所以我们就去了。我们去得早，也回来得早。所以我不知道你在做什么。所以这是我。就是我。我在这里。没错。在这儿。

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:18.820
<v Sean Cole>C'est moi. That's "it's me" in French. Sometimes there's not much more than that.
<v Sean Cole>“C'est moi”（这是我）。那是法语里的“就是我”。有时除了这个没有更多的了。

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:22.310
<v Sean's Mom>C'est moi. Love you lots. Talk to you soon. Bye.
<v Sean's Mom>就是我。非常爱你。很快再跟你聊。再见。

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:23.440
<v Sean Cole>This one's even shorter.
<v Sean Cole>这个更短。

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.610
<v Sean's Mom>C'est moi. Bye.
<v Sean's Mom>就是我。再见。

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:33.630
<v Sean Cole>But of course, all the messages boil down to her saying, in one creative way or another, please call me back. Sometimes she was a lot more direct about it.
<v Sean Cole>但当然，所有的信息归根结底都是她在以某种创造性的方式说，请回电话给我。有时她要直接得多。

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:43.460
<v Sean's Mom>(SLOWLY) Don't forget to call your mother. Goodbye.
<v Sean's Mom>（缓慢地）别忘了给你妈妈打电话。再见。

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:51.480
<v Sean Cole>Actually, here's the one that I always tell people about when I'm talking about her messages. It's like she sort of circles and then goes in for the kill.
<v Sean Cole>事实上，这是我每次谈论她的留言时都会告诉人们的那一条。就像她先绕个圈子，然后直接切入正题。

00:23:51.480 --> 00:24:16.970
<v Sean's Mom>I heard you on the radio on Sunday, and I knew it was you because I can recognize your voice, even though it's been a long time since I've heard it on the phone. So anyway, I love you lots and lots. Call me when you think of it, if you ever do. Love you. Bye.
<v Sean's Mom>我在周日的广播里听到了你的声音，我知道是你，因为我能认出你的声音，尽管我已经很久没有在电话里听过它了。所以，总之，我非常爱你。当你想到的时候给我打个电话，如果你真的会想的话。爱你。再见。

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:40.220
<v Sean Cole>My mom-- her name was Pat-- she died on September 28, 2015 at 4:22 in the morning. It was relatively sudden and totally unexpected. And as much as I thought I was preparing myself for that moment, I wasn't prepared. It's true I didn't call enough, but she was still the first person I thought to call when something huge happened, good or bad.
<v Sean Cole>我妈妈——她叫帕特——她在2015年9月28日凌晨4点22分去世了。这相对突然且完全出乎意料。尽管我以为我在为那一刻做准备，但我并没有准备好。确实我打的电话不够多，但当发生什么大事时，好或坏，她仍然是我第一个想到要打电话的人。

00:24:40.220 --> 00:25:01.540
<v Sean Cole>I loved talking to her. She was funny, as you can tell, and smart. She wrote technical manuals in the early days of personal computing. Later in life, she lugged a lot of pro-grade camera equipment around the world, taking pictures. And yet, she had a hard time figuring out her smartphone. The last recording I have from her saved on my phone was a pocket dial.
<v Sean Cole>我喜欢跟她说话。正如你所看到的，她很幽默，也很聪明。她在个人计算的早期编写技术手册。后来在生活中，她背着很多专业级的摄影器材满世界拍照。然而，她却很难弄明白她的智能手机。我手机里保存的她最后一段录音是一个误触的口袋电话。

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:04.370
<v Sean Cole>[SCUFFLING]
<v Sean Cole>[摩擦声]

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:06.290
<v Sean Cole>This goes on for three minutes.
<v Sean Cole>这持续了三分钟。

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:11.120
<v Sean Cole>[SCUFFLING CONTINUES]
<v Sean Cole>[摩擦声继续]

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:12.980
<v Sean Cole>Did you go for your walk this morning?
<v Sean Cole>你今天早上去散步了吗？

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:17.330
<v Ed Hacker>No, I didn't make it this morning. I figured that I was pretty busy.
<v Ed Hacker>没有，我今天早上没去。我觉得我挺忙的。

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:37.600
<v Sean Cole>After Mom died, I started calling home a lot more to talk to my stepdad, Ed Hacker. He and I never really had a phone relationship when my mother was alive. It was more the classic thing of, you want to say hi to Ed? And then we'd verbally clap each other on the back and then back to Mom. These days, it's not weird for us to spend almost two hours on the phone together.
<v Sean Cole>妈妈去世后，我开始更频繁地打电话回家，跟我的继父埃德·哈克聊天。我妈妈在世时，我和他并没有真正的电话关系。更多是那种经典的，“你想跟埃德打个招呼吗？”然后我们口头上互相拍拍后背，然后又转回妈妈那。如今，我们进行两个小时的电话交谈已经不再奇怪了。

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.090
<v Ed Hacker>Then I had to give tweets out, so--
<v Ed Hacker>然后我不得不发推文，所以——

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:44.390
<v Sean Cole>You are the most active octogenarian on Twitter that I know about.
<v Sean Cole>你是我认识的最活跃的八旬Twitter用户。

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:47.300
<v Ed Hacker>Well, I try to send out about six or seven tweets.
<v Ed Hacker>嗯，我尽量发六七条推文。

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:49.270
<v Sean Cole>What did you send this morning?
<v Sean Cole>你今天早上发了什么？

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:51.380
<v Ed Hacker>Oh, I sent about three or four from--
<v Ed Hacker>哦，我发了三四条关于——

00:25:51.380 --> 00:26:13.130
<v Sean Cole>I think of how annoyed my mom would be if she knew this, but I'm now performing the exact telephone behavior she wanted from me when she was around, except now with Ed. I call about once a week on average, and it's always me initiating the calls now. At first, of course, it was mainly to make sure he was holding up OK and to make sure I was holding up OK.
<v Sean Cole>我想如果我妈妈知道这件事会多厌烦，但我现在正在做她还在世时希望我做的完全相同的电话行为，只不过现在对象换成了埃德。平均每周我打一次电话，现在都是我主动发起的。起初，当然，主要是为了确保他一切都好，也确保我自己一切都好。

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:30.470
<v Sean Cole>But even now, when I miss a week, it eats at me. I'm thinking, got to call Ed, got to call Ed. It's like an injustice that he's getting this treatment and not her, and I keep trying to square it somehow. But when I put this to Ed, he basically said it's really not that big a mystery.
<v Sean Cole>但即使是现在，当我错过了一周时，这也会困扰我。我想，得给埃德打电话，得给埃德打电话。他受到这种待遇而不是她，这感觉像是不公正，我一直试图以某种方式平复它。但当我把这一点告诉埃德时，他基本上说这真的不是什么大谜团。

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:48.400
<v Ed Hacker>Kind of obvious that if one parent dies, you realize that the other one may not be that far off, that he will go too, or she. So the scarcity, just like in economics, makes the value go up.
<v Ed Hacker>有点显而易见，如果一位父母去世了，你会意识到另一位可能离那也不远了，他也将离开，或者是她。所以稀缺性，就像在经济学中一样，使价值上升。

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:50.900
<v Sean Cole>I never thought of this in economic terms before.
<v Sean Cole>我以前从来没从经济学的角度考虑过这一点。

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:56.450
<v Ed Hacker>Well, it's true of many things. If the population is very low--
<v Ed Hacker>嗯，许多事情都是这样。如果人口非常少——

00:26:56.450 --> 00:27:14.270
<v Sean Cole>Ed's 87. He taught philosophy and logic at a university in Boston for a lot of years, which is fitting because Ed is very logical and philosophical. He's always quoting one or another great thinker. For fun, he does math. He and my mom got together when I was six. He moved in when I was 11.
<v Sean Cole>埃德87岁了。他在波士顿的一所大学教了很多年的哲学和逻辑，这很合适，因为埃德非常有逻辑且有哲学头脑。他总是引用某位伟大的思想家的话。为了消遣，他做数学题。我和妈妈在我六岁时在一起。他在我11岁时搬进来。

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:36.680
<v Sean Cole>And I think he's right that I call because I'm more aware than ever that one day he won't be on the other end of the phone, but it's more than that too. Even though we have all of these other people in our family whom we love, it got to feeling like Ed's the other one Mom's death happened to, like he and I were the ones who mutually needed to talk about Mom and to hear about her.
<v Sean Cole>而且我认为他是对的，我打电话是因为我比以往任何时候都更清楚，有一天他不会在电话的另一端，但也不仅限于此。即使我们在我们的家庭中还有所有其他我们爱的人，这开始让人觉得埃德是妈妈去世后的那个“幸存者”，就像他和我是那些必须互相交流关于妈妈的事情，并听到关于她的一些事情的人。

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:44.480
<v Ed Hacker>I feel that, yes, I can talk to you about Pat because you're willing to talk to her about it.
<v Ed Hacker>我感觉到了，是的，我可以跟你谈论帕特，因为你愿意谈论她。

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:45.880
<v Sean Cole>To you about it, yeah.
<v Sean Cole>跟你谈论这件事，是的。

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:47.930
<v Ed Hacker>And it makes a big difference to me.
<v Ed Hacker>这对我有很大影响。

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:48.920
<v Sean Cole>It does?
<v Sean Cole>真的吗？

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:49.670
<v Ed Hacker>Of course.
<v Ed Hacker>当然。

00:27:49.670 --> 00:27:51.650
<v Sean Cole>I'm so glad to hear that.
<v Sean Cole>我很高兴听到这个。

00:27:51.650 --> 00:28:12.640
<v Ed Hacker>Because you lost the same person, even if it's somewhat similar if somebody else has lost someone else, like these groups in which everyone has lost a spouse. And their memories are different. You never met that other person that died, and you really don't care. I mean--
<v Ed Hacker>因为你失去了同一个人，即使如果其他人失去了别人，类似于这些每个人都失去了配偶的团体，情况有点相似。他们的记忆是不同的。你从来没见过那个死去的其他人，而且你真的不在乎。我是说——

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:15.250
<v Sean Cole>[LAUGHS]
<v Sean Cole>[笑声]

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:17.560
<v Ed Hacker>You care about your loss. I mean, you know.
<v Ed Hacker>你在乎的是你的损失。我是说，你知道的。

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:18.060
<v Sean Cole>Yeah.
<v Sean Cole>是的。

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:20.150
<v Ed Hacker>Let's be honest.
<v Ed Hacker>说实话。

00:28:20.150 --> 00:28:21.800
<v Sean Cole>Are you on the deck now?
<v Sean Cole>你现在在露台上吗？

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:23.030
<v Ed Hacker>I am.
<v Ed Hacker>是的。

00:28:23.030 --> 00:28:24.150
<v Sean Cole>Having a smoke?
<v Sean Cole>在抽烟？

00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:30.290
<v Ed Hacker>I am.
<v Ed Hacker>是的。

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:53.180
<v Sean Cole>For a long time, my mom was a third invisible person in all of these conversations, but we've both noticed we're talking about mom less and less. People told me this would happen, but I didn't really believe them, or I didn't want to. Time slouches on. You wake up to different thoughts in the morning. And when you call home, the first thing you say isn't, how are you holding up?
<v Sean Cole>很长一段时间里，我妈妈在所有这些对话中都是第三个隐形人，但我们俩都注意到我们谈论妈妈越来越少了。人们告诉我这会发生，但我并不真正相信，或者我不愿意相信。时间缓缓流逝。你早上带着不同的思绪醒来。当你打电话回家时，你说的第一句话不再是，“你还好吗？”

00:28:53.180 --> 00:29:03.380
<v Sean Cole>It used to be that you would kind of mark how long it had been. You'd say, I can't believe it's been three-- it'll be three months on Monday, or I can't believe it'll be nine months on Thursday or whatever.
<v Sean Cole>过去通常你会标记已经过了多久。你会说，我真不敢相信已经三个月了——周一就满三个月了，或者我真不敢相信周四就九个月了，诸如此类的。

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:05.750
<v Ed Hacker>Well, I still keep track of the time.
<v Ed Hacker>嗯，我还在记录时间。

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:06.770
<v Sean Cole>You do?
<v Sean Cole>你还在记录吗？

00:29:06.770 --> 00:29:17.910
<v Ed Hacker>Yeah. And I also have an app on my computer, all three of them, which is set to tell me how many days since Pat died.
<v Ed Hacker>是的。我电脑上还有一个应用程序，三个都在上面，它们被设置为告诉我帕特去世多少天了。

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:19.020
<v Sean Cole>You do?
<v Sean Cole>真的吗？

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:19.770
<v Ed Hacker>I do.
<v Ed Hacker>是的。

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:20.770
<v Sean Cole>I didn't know that.
<v Sean Cole>我不知道。

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:43.970
<v Ed Hacker>So this way-- I'm going there right now to see what my apps are. Well, it's been 22 months and three days since she's died. Or if you want, one year, 10 months, and three days. Or if you want, 96 weeks.
<v Ed Hacker>所以这样——我现在就要去看看我的应用程序。嗯，帕特去世已经22个月零3天了。或者如果你想要的话，一年零10个月零3天。或者如果你想要的话，96周。

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:47.780
<v Sean Cole>And what do you feel like, looking at that?
<v Sean Cole>看着那个，你有什么感觉？

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:54.200
<v Ed Hacker>Don't have any particular feeling. It's just that it's amazing that it's almost two years.
<v Ed Hacker>没有什么特别的感觉。只是令人惊讶的是快两年了。

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:55.310
<v Sean Cole>Hm.
<v Sean Cole>嗯。

00:29:55.310 --> 00:29:58.880
<v Ed Hacker>It's like saying, I remember.
<v Ed Hacker>就像在说，我记得。

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:09.320
<v Sean Cole>Yeah, I feel like I need something like that, some sort of-- I just feel like I don't think about her enough.
<v Sean Cole>是的，我觉得我需要像那样的东西，某种——我只是觉得我想她的时间不够多。

00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:13.820
<v Ed Hacker>Well, that you'll have to explain. Why should you think more?
<v Ed Hacker>嗯，那得由你来解释了。为什么你应该想得更多？

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:15.710
<v Sean Cole>I don't-- it's just like--
<v Sean Cole>我不——这就像——

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:17.210
<v Ed Hacker>Is it guilt or something?
<v Ed Hacker>是因为内疚或者什么吗？

00:30:17.210 --> 00:30:18.820
<v Sean Cole>It's not guilt, no. It's--
<v Sean Cole>不是内疚，不是。这是——

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:20.390
<v Ed Hacker>You feel like you'll honor her more by thinking about her?
<v Ed Hacker>你觉得通过想她，你会更尊重她吗？

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:40.640
<v Sean Cole>It's like, yeah, I want to honor her more by thinking about her, and it also feels like there's something going on in me all the time that I'm not acknowledging that leaks out in these other ways. And I just miss her, and so it's like I need to put that missing somewhere.
<v Sean Cole>这就像是，是的，我想通过想她来更尊重她，而且也觉得我身体里一直有些东西在发生，我没有意识到，以其他方式泄露出来。我只是想念她，所以感觉我需要把这种想念放在什么地方。

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:45.500
<v Ed Hacker>Well, you have a photograph of Pat?
<v Ed Hacker>嗯，你有帕特的照片吗？

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:48.760
<v Sean Cole>Yeah. I have one up on the wall in my office.
<v Sean Cole>有的。我办公室墙上有一张。

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:57.330
<v Ed Hacker>OK. Take another one. And every day, move it from one spot to another in your apartment.
<v Ed Hacker>好的。再拿一张。每天，在你的公寓里把它从一个地方移到另一个地方。

00:30:57.330 --> 00:31:01.150
<v Sean Cole>That's a really good idea. Did you just think of that?
<v Sean Cole>那是个非常好的主意。你刚才想到的吗？

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:12.700
<v Ed Hacker>Yes. That makes it sort of a ritual.
<v Ed Hacker>是的。这让它成为一种仪式。

00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:48.540
<v Sean Cole>But the truth is I already have the ritual I need. I don't do it every day, but I do it just about every week. I call Ed. We talk. For this specific need I have, it turns out he's the perfect person to call. Maybe you've got somebody like that, a personal Ghostbuster when there's something strange in the neighborhood, when things are looking their worst, that person who will know what you're talking about, even if they can't understand what you're saying. And all you got to do is call.
<v Sean Cole>但事实是我已经有了我需要的仪式。我不是每天都做，但我几乎每周都做。我给埃德打电话。我们交谈。对于我有的这个具体需要，事实证明他是最完美的人选。也许你也有这样的人，一个在你身边发生奇怪的事情、情况最糟糕的时候的个人“捉鬼敢死队”，那个会知道你在说什么的人，即使他们无法理解你在说什么。你所要做的就是打电话。

00:31:48.540 --> 00:32:00.130
<v Ira Glass>Sean Cole. When he checked in with Ed this week, Ed was the same as always and doing well. He turns 90 in April.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩·科尔。他本周与埃德通了电话，埃德一如既往，过得很好。他四月就90岁了。

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:09.920
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "Mom." OK, so this is a story we first put on the air in 1996. And yes, our program has been on the air for a long time.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“妈妈”。好吧，这是一个我们在1996年首次播出的故事。是的，我们的节目已经播出了很长时间。

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:26.830
<v Ira Glass>Diane, who's putting together this part of the show with me, producer Diane Wu, she listened to this recording, and she told me that I sound exactly the same in it as I sound today, which-- I don't know. Is that a good thing? Is that not a good thing? Shouldn't a person grow? I think I should sound at least a little different.
<v Ira Glass>黛安，正在和我一起整理节目这一部分的制片人黛安·吴，她听了这段录音，告诉我我在里面听起来和今天一模一样，这——我不知道。这是好事吗？这是坏事吗？一个人不应该成长吗？我想我听起来至少应该有点不同。

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:37.450
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, here is how I introduced this story back then in 1996. Quick warning, before the story starts, that this story acknowledges the existence of sex. OK, here we go.
<v Ira Glass>总之，这就是我在1996年介绍这个故事的方式。快速警告，在故事开始之前，这个故事承认性的存在。好吧，我们要开始了。

00:32:37.450 --> 00:33:02.640
<v Ira Glass>Our parents can surprise us with what they don't tell us, with what they don't talk about, especially when it comes to sex. Recently I had this experience. An ex-girlfriend was in the gym, looking through a copy of Marie Claire magazine, women's magazine. And there was an article in it on women's fantasies, their sexual fantasies-- what do your man's dirty daydreams reveal about what he wants from you?
<v Ira Glass>我们的父母在他们不告诉我们的事情、他们不谈论的事情方面会让我们惊讶，尤其是在性方面。最近我有过这种经历。一个前女友在健身房里，正在看一本《嘉人》（Marie Claire）杂志，女性杂志。里面有一篇文章是关于女性幻想的，她们的性幻想——你男人的肮脏白日梦揭示了他想要从你那里得到什么？

00:33:02.640 --> 00:33:22.030
<v Ira Glass>In the article, six sexperts-- that was the word they used, "sexperts"-- reveal the six most common male sex fantasy scenarios. So my ex-girlfriend is reading. And there, in the third paragraph, one of the sexperts turns out to be my mother.
<v Ira Glass>在那篇文章中，六位性专家——这是他们用的词，“性专家”（sexperts）——揭示了六种最常见的男性性幻想场景。所以我的前女友正在读。在那里，在第三段，其中一位性专家竟然是我妈妈。

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:26.910
<v Ira Glass>[DIAL TONE]
<v Ira Glass>[拨号音]

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:27.630
<v Shirley Glass>Hello?
<v Shirley Glass>喂？

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:28.260
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Mom?
