00:40:56.760 --> 00:41:23.040
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, with kids out of school all over the country and businesses closed, and people hunkered down at home with family, and also lots of people far from family and the ones they love, reaching out and worried for them, we have put together a show of family stories that we've made over the years. Both the stories in this next part of our show are people turning to their dads over the telephone with a question. This first one, Act Four, "It Takes a Villa," comes from Neil Drumming.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，全国各地的孩子都放假了，企业关闭了，人们缩在家里与家人在一起，而且有很多人远离家人和他们所爱的人，联系他们并为他们担心，我们整合了一期关于家庭故事的节目，这些故事是我们多年来制作的。在我们节目接下来的这一部分中，两个故事都是关于人们通过电话向他们的父亲询问问题。第一个，第四幕，“租个别墅”，来自尼尔·德拉明。

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:49.350
<v Neil Drumming>In the summer of 1982, my dad did something unexpected, something that seemed unbelievably indulgent. He took me, my mom, my brother, and the youngest of my three sisters on the most epic road trip any of us could have possibly imagined at the time. We piled into my dad's Buick Skylark and drove from Queens, New York to the World's Fair in Knoxville, Tennessee, where a robot danced for us, and then down into Orlando, Florida.
<v Neil Drumming>1982年的夏天，我爸爸做了一些意想不到的事情，一些看起来令人难以置信地放纵的事情。他带着我、我妈妈、我哥哥以及我三个妹妹中最小的一个，踏上了我们在当时能想象到的最史诗般的公路旅行。我们挤进爸爸的别克Skylark车里，从纽约皇后区开车到田纳西州诺克斯维尔的世界博览会，那里有一个机器人在为我们跳舞，然后一直开到佛罗里达州奥兰多。

00:41:49.350 --> 00:42:13.830
<v Neil Drumming>This was a big deal. Before this, going away meant visiting relatives in South Carolina and sitting uncomfortably among aunts and second cousins whose names I would forget before we'd even pulled out of their dusty driveways. This trip was not country heat and sipping sugar water on some rickety porch while listening to the inscrutable conversation of grown folk. It was what going 1,000 miles from home should feel like.
<v Neil Drumming>这是一件大事。在此之前，出门意味着去南卡罗来纳州看亲戚，坐在阿姨和二表亲中间感到不自在，而我在离开他们尘土飞扬的私道前就会忘记他们的名字。这次旅行不是乡村热，不是在破旧的门廊上喝糖水，同时听着大人们不可理解的谈话。这就是离家1000英里应该有的感觉。

00:42:13.830 --> 00:42:27.400
<v Neil Drumming>We cruised down a brightly-lit street called International Drive. We stayed at a Holiday Inn taller and more grand than any I'd ever seen. Sunlight streamed in through a hole in the ceiling, a hole that was supposed to be there.
<v Neil Drumming>我们沿着一条灯火通明的街道叫国际大道行驶。我们住进了一家比我见过的任何一家都更高大、更宏伟的假日酒店。阳光从天花板的一个洞里流进来，一个应该就在那里的洞。

00:42:27.400 --> 00:43:02.220
<v Neil Drumming>Our parents took us to a building that looked like a pile of poached eggs, but was actually Xanadu, House of the Future. And everywhere, along every roadside, billboards promised that the most magical scene still awaited us, this place, Disney World. By all accounts, it was paradise for kids. But between the gas, and the hotels, and the eating out, my dad quickly discovered how expensive taking even 60% of his brood on a Disney vacation could be. He was resigned to do it, but he wasn't above working the angles.
<v Neil Drumming>父母带我们去了一栋看起来像一堆水煮蛋的建筑，但实际上是Xanadu，未来之家。到处，沿着每一条路边，广告牌都承诺最神奇的景象仍然在等着我们，这个地方，迪士尼世界。根据所有的叙述，这对孩子们来说是天堂。但汽油、酒店和在外就餐的费用加起来，爸爸很快发现带着他后代中哪怕60%去迪士尼度假是多么昂贵。他认命去做，但他并不排斥“运作”一下。

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:37.990
<v Neil Drumming>He found out that you could get cheap tickets to the Magic Kingdom if you just signed up to sit through an hour or so spiel from someone pitching timeshares. He was in. The hard sell went down at the Disney Village, a branded mini mall near the famous theme park. My mom, dad, and a handful of other determined parents stowed their kids in a room full of toys that had been conveniently provided by the salespeople. The parents set about the business of listening, or not, waiting patiently for the moment when the closers would stop shilling and start handing out the Disney discounts.
<v Neil Drumming>他发现如果你只是报名参加一个小时左右推销分时度假房的人的演讲，你就可以以便宜的价格买到魔法王国的门票。他加入了。那场硬推销发生在迪士尼村，一个著名的主题公园附近带品牌的迷你购物中心。我妈妈、爸爸和少数其他意志坚定的家长把他们的孩子关在销售人员方便提供的装满玩具的房间里。家长们开始听，或者不听，耐心地等待推销员停止兜售并开始分发迪士尼折扣的时候。

00:43:37.990 --> 00:44:00.500
<v Neil Drumming>But while we kids were in another room throwing LEGOs at one another, something surprising happened. My dad bit. He went into a closed room to get three-day passes just so that I could eventually lose my glasses on Space Mountain, and he came out with a deed, the deed to something he and my mom were now calling our villa.
<v Neil Drumming>但当我们在另一个房间里用乐高积木互相投掷时，发生了令人惊讶的事情。我爸爸上钩了。他走进一个封闭的房间去买三天通票，只是为了让我最终能在太空山上弄丢我的眼镜，而他出来时拿着一份契约，一份他们现在称之为“我们的别墅”的东西的契约。

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:20.870
<v Neil Drumming>My father was a bold man, but in retrospect this is the most impetuous action that I have ever seen him take. It cost him about $5,000, which he paid in installments. In 1982, for a guy with five kids, who never made more than $33,000 a year at his day job, it was a considerable investment.
<v Neil Drumming>我父亲是一个勇敢的人，但回想起来，这是我见过的他采取的最冲动的行为。它花了他大约5000美元，他分期付款。在1982年，对于一个有五个孩子、在日常工作中从未赚超过33000美元一年的家伙来说，这是一笔相当大的投资。

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:46.070
<v Neil Drumming>For those unfamiliar with timeshares, it may be hard to wrap your head around buying a vacation home that you never really own. You pay upfront for it. There's an annual maintenance fee, but you only get to stay in it once a year or so, usually for a week at a time. It almost sounds like some sort of scam, and sometimes it is. But it didn't turn out that way for us. Instead, it became a fixture in my family.
<v Neil Drumming>对于那些不熟悉分时度假房的人来说，可能很难理解购买一个你从未真正拥有的度假屋。你预付钱。有年度维护费，但你每年只去住一次左右，通常是一次一周。它听起来几乎像某种骗局，有时确实如此。但对我们来说并没有那样。相反，它成了我家里的固定节目。

00:44:46.070 --> 00:45:16.070
<v Neil Drumming>My father had chosen, as our week, the first week in July. And so every year, during one of the hottest months of the year, we would head down I-95 as always. But now when we pulled into South Carolina to see relatives, that was only a pit stop on the way to our true destination. We had transformed from people who went away to a family who went on vacation.
<v Neil Drumming>我父亲选择的我们那一周是7月的第一个星期。所以每年，在一年中最热的月份之一，我们就像往常一样沿着95号州际公路南下。但现在当我们开车去南卡罗来纳州看亲戚时，那只是前往我们真正目的地的一站。我们已经从去度假的人转变为一个去度假的家庭。

00:45:16.070 --> 00:45:42.140
<v Neil Drumming>Our villa was number 317, a two-bedroom apartment with an enclosed back porch that looked out onto a small man-made lake complete with fish, ducks, and another summer word that I learned, "gazebo." My brother chased cicadas and lizards. For my sister, the only swimmer among my siblings, there was a pool. There were tennis courts and bikes to rent. The general store even offered a collection of the latest movies on laser disk.
<v Neil Drumming>我们的别墅是317号，一个带封闭后门廊的两居室公寓，俯瞰着一个人工小湖，里面有鱼、鸭子，还有一个我学到的夏天的词，“凉亭”（gazebo）。我哥哥追逐蝉和蜥蜴。对于我姐妹中唯一一个游泳的人来说，有一个游泳池。有网球场和可供租用的自行车。杂货店甚至提供了一系列激光影碟上的最新电影。

00:45:42.140 --> 00:45:59.720
<v Neil Drumming>That first trip, I was eight. As I got older, I moved from the gazebo to the game room and then the gym, trying to meet other kids my age. My mom busied herself in the kitchen, making lunches, or sat by the lake and watched the ducks. My dad shepherded us through It's a Small World and Epcot Center.
<v Neil Drumming>那第一次旅行，我八岁。随着我长大，我从凉亭搬到了游戏室，然后是健身房，试图结识和我同龄的其他孩子。我妈妈忙着在厨房里做午餐，或者坐在湖边看鸭子。爸爸带领我们游览了“世界是个小地方”（It's a Small World）和艾波卡特中心（Epcot Center）。

00:45:59.720 --> 00:46:15.950
<v Neil Drumming>Our summers went on like this, pretty much exactly like this, probably until I finished high school. I honestly loved it. I looked forward to this trip every year. And even though it was only a week, it was almost always the highlight of my entire summer.
<v Neil Drumming>我们的夏天就是这样过的，几乎完全就是这样，可能直到我高中毕业。我真心喜欢它。我每年都盼望着这次旅行。即使它只有一周，也几乎总是整个夏天最精彩的亮点。

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:31.840
<v Neil Drumming>But when I think about it now, it occurs to me my dad pretty much orchestrated this thing that became so important to our lives, and I have no idea whether or not he ever enjoyed it himself. In fact, it didn't seem like he did. I can't recall actually seeing him happy.
<v Neil Drumming>但现在想起它时，我想到爸爸几乎策划了这个对我们生活变得如此重要的事情，而我根本不知道他自己是否曾享受过它。事实上，看起来他并没有。我不记得曾真正看到他快乐过。

00:46:31.840 --> 00:47:00.350
<v Neil Drumming>Neither does my brother. He says Dad was pretty much the same at the timeshare as he was at home. Sometimes he'd go for walks alone, but often he just sat on the couch and watched TV. I asked my sister. She said he must have been happy, but she doesn't remember witnessing it either. It seems like such a simple question, but I just wanted to know, did he enjoy himself?
<v Neil Drumming>我哥哥也不记得。他说爸爸在分时度假屋时几乎和在家时一样。有时他会独自去散步，但通常他只是坐在沙发上看电视。我问了我的姐妹。她说他一定很快乐，但她也不记得见过他快乐。这看起来是一个如此简单的问题，但我只是想知道，他自己享受了吗？

00:47:00.350 --> 00:47:21.550
<v Neil Drumming>At the risk of embodying the most tired trope in all of modern masculinity, I will say my father and I never really got along. He was strict, his house had a lot of rules, and he believed in corporal punishment. And the sting of that conflict stayed with me as an adult. But since my mom passed away last year, I've been trying to connect with him more. I gave him a call.
<v Neil Drumming>冒着体现现代男性气质中最令人厌倦的陈词滥调的风险，我会说我和父亲从来没有真正相处融洽。他很严厉，他的房子有很多规则，他相信体罚。那种冲突的刺痛感一直伴随着我进入成年。但自从我妈妈去年去世后，我一直试图更多地与他联系。我给他打了个电话。

00:47:21.550 --> 00:47:25.730
<v Neil Drumming>[DIAL TONE]
<v Neil Drumming>[拨号音]

00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:26.660
<v Neil's Dad>Hello?
<v Neil's Dad>喂？

00:47:26.660 --> 00:47:27.750
<v Neil Drumming>Hello?
<v Neil Drumming>喂？

00:47:27.750 --> 00:47:28.500
<v Neil's Dad>Yes?
<v Neil's Dad>是的？

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:29.580
<v Neil Drumming>Hey, it's Neil.
<v Neil Drumming>嘿，我是尼尔。

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:30.630
<v Neil's Dad>Yes.
<v Neil's Dad>是的。

00:47:30.630 --> 00:47:31.800
<v Neil Drumming>Is it a bad time?
<v Neil Drumming>现在方便吗？

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:33.020
<v Neil's Dad>It's about who?
<v Neil's Dad>你是谁？

00:47:33.020 --> 00:47:34.600
<v Neil Drumming>I said, is it a bad time?
<v Neil Drumming>我说，现在方便吗？

00:47:34.600 --> 00:47:43.180
<v Neil's Dad>No, no. I was just playing solitaire. Yeah. I didn't know whether it was the drugstore or not.
<v Neil's Dad>不，不。我刚才在玩纸牌。是的。我不知道这是不是药房打来的。

00:47:43.180 --> 00:47:45.540
<v Neil Drumming>Are you waiting for a call from the drugstore?
<v Neil Drumming>你在等药房的电话吗？

00:47:45.540 --> 00:47:50.250
<v Neil's Dad>No, they'll call. They'll give me a call no matter when it is.
<v Neil's Dad>不，他们会打来的。无论什么时候他们都会给我打电话的。

00:47:50.250 --> 00:48:05.040
<v Neil Drumming>My dad is 83 years old now and living alone in Florida. Talking to him can be awkward and not just because his hearing is going. I asked him point blank if he liked going to the villa. He told me that when he was growing up, he barely ever left South Carolina.
<v Neil Drumming>我爸爸现在83岁了，独自住在佛罗里达州。和他说话可能会很尴尬，不只是因为他的听力下降了。我直截了当地问他是否喜欢去别墅。他告诉我，当他成长时，他几乎从没离开过南卡罗来纳州。

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:11.620
<v Neil's Dad>I didn't know nothing about nothing else. You saw things in magazines and stuff.
<v Neil's Dad>我对其他任何事情都一无所知。你从杂志上看到各种东西。

00:48:11.620 --> 00:48:12.940
<v Neil Drumming>Mhm.
<v Neil Drumming>嗯哼。

00:48:12.940 --> 00:48:18.260
<v Neil's Dad>The first time I knew about a dentist, I was in the Army.
<v Neil's Dad>我第一次知道牙医是在我参军的时候。

00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:21.520
<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHS]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑声]

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:32.050
<v Neil's Dad>But I just thought it was a good idea that our kids see something other than their surroundings and where they were born.
<v Neil's Dad>但我只是觉得让我们的孩子看到除了他们周围环境和他们出生地之外的东西是个好主意。

00:48:32.050 --> 00:48:33.650
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。

00:48:33.650 --> 00:48:50.480
<v Ira Glass>My dad grew up poor on a farm, one of 12 children. He says he only finished high school because by the time he was old enough, he was the one driving the bus. Sometimes when there were athletic events at other schools, he'd get to drive the teams and learn what the nearby towns were like.
<v Ira Glass>我爸爸是在农场里贫穷长大的，是12个孩子之一。他说他只读完了高中，因为当他大到那个年纪时，他就是开校车的人。有时当其他学校有体育赛事时，他会去开校车运送球队，了解附近的城镇是什么样子的。

00:48:50.480 --> 00:49:12.340
<v Ira Glass>In 1953, he was drafted into the Army, which had only recently been integrated. They sent him to Colorado and Indiana, and it wasn't great. He says the Army was really not into black people back then. So those were his travel experiences when he was young. I was hearing a lot of this for the first time, and as it turns out, that's at least partially my own fault.
<v Ira Glass>1953年，他被征入伍，军队最近才实现种族融合。他们把他送到科罗拉多州和印第安纳州，情况并不太好。他说军队那时候确实不喜欢黑人。所以那是他年轻时的旅行经历。我第一次听到很多这样的事情，事实证明，那至少部分归咎于我自己。

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:20.530
<v Neil's Dad>The reason why we never talk about it, because it just wasn't the kind of thing that you guys seemed to be interested in.
<v Neil's Dad>我们从不谈论它的原因，是因为这看起来不是你们感兴趣的那种事情。

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:24.850
<v Neil Drumming>Really? So we just didn't seem like we were interested as kids?
<v Neil Drumming>真的吗？所以我们小的时候看起来对它不感兴趣？

00:49:24.850 --> 00:49:26.210
<v Neil's Dad>Yeah, right.
<v Neil's Dad>是的，没错。

00:49:26.210 --> 00:49:59.680
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, I probably wasn't so interested back then, back when the two of us were constantly challenging each other. I was always either afraid of him or angry at him, hiding from him, or planting my feet to confront him. It never crossed my mind to try to understand him. But nowadays, my dad feels to me like some kind of living cold case, a million-page brief that is no longer redacted. Maybe it's because I'm now at the age he was when I was born, but I retroactively find his every decision fascinating, even the ones that aren't so surprising on the surface.
<v Neil Drumming>是的，我那时可能确实没那么感兴趣，当时我们俩不断地互相挑战。我一直要么怕他，要么生他的气，躲着他，或者站稳脚跟去面对他。理解他从来没有跨过我的脑海。但如今，我爸爸给我的感觉就像某种活着的“冷案”，一份不再被删减的百万页简报。也许是因为我现在到了他生我时的年纪，但我回顾性地发现他每一个决定都很迷人，即使是那些表面上看起来并不那么令人惊讶的决定。

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:02.240
<v Neil Drumming>Why Florida?
<v Neil Drumming>为什么选择佛罗里达？

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:20.170
<v Neil's Dad>It was advertising. You get to hear something about Florida and then this thing, Disney World. After we started going, they built Epcot. They built Animal Kingdom.
<v Neil's Dad>那是广告。你从佛罗里达听说了一些事情，然后这个东西，迪士尼世界。我们开始去之后，他们建了艾波卡特中心。他们建了动物王国。

00:50:20.170 --> 00:50:20.770
<v Neil Drumming>Mhm.
<v Neil Drumming>嗯哼。

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:22.820
<v Neil's Dad>And they advertise them a lot.
<v Neil's Dad>而且他们做了很多广告。

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:23.470
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。

00:50:23.470 --> 00:50:31.330
<v Neil's Dad>Not many people were going. We were probably the most vacationing people in our area.
<v Neil's Dad>没有多少人去。我们可能是我们地区度假次数最多的人。

00:50:31.330 --> 00:50:31.930
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。

00:50:31.930 --> 00:50:38.080
<v Neil's Dad>I don't know of any other family that went on vacation every year. We did.
<v Neil's Dad>我不知道还有哪个家庭每年都去度假。我们去了。

00:50:38.080 --> 00:51:05.930
<v Neil Drumming>My dad was obviously proud that he'd gotten the timeshare, but pride, strictly speaking, does not constitute joy. It didn't answer the question of whether or not he was actually happy spending those summer weeks with us at the villa. Instead, he kept trying to make me understand why he brought us there in the first place. And his explanation, his reasoning reach back to memories and past experiences that not only had I never heard, but that kind of blew my mind.
<v Neil Drumming>我爸爸显然为他买了分时度假房感到自豪，但严格来说，自豪并不构成快乐。这没有回答他是否真的很高兴和我们在别墅度过那些夏天的几周。相反，他一直在试图让我明白他为什么要带我们去那里。而他的解释、他的推理追溯到了一些我不但从没听说过，而且让我大开眼界的记忆和过去的经历。

00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:45.090
<v Neil's Dad>I tell you, probably where I got the whole idea-- when we were in school, every summer you had to try to think of something that you could write about when you'd go back to school, because you're going to have to write something about what you did this summer. Well, we never had anything to write about when I was going to school. And you didn't think plowing a mule, or picking peaches, or stuff that you had to normally do-- you didn't think that was so exciting to write about.
<v Neil's Dad>我告诉你，我整个想法可能来自这里——当我们在学校时，每个夏天你都得尝试想出一些你可以写的东西，当你回到学校时，因为你回到学校必须写写你这个夏天做了什么。嗯，我上学时从来没有什么可写的。而且你不会想到耕驴，或者摘桃子，或者你通常必须做的那些事情——你不觉得那有多令人兴奋可以写下来。

00:51:45.090 --> 00:51:48.010
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。

00:51:48.010 --> 00:52:04.710
<v Neil's Dad>So we made up lies about what we did. Well, every summer you guys went on vacation, you could write about something that you did, or saw, or someplace you went.
<v Neil's Dad>所以我们编造了关于我们做了什么的谎言。嗯，每个夏天你们都去度假，你们可以写关于你们做过、看过或去过的地方的事情。

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:10.220
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah. What did you do during the summers?
<v Neil Drumming>是的。你夏天做什么？

00:52:10.220 --> 00:52:12.110
<v Neil's Dad>When, this year?
<v Neil's Dad>嗯，今年？

00:52:12.110 --> 00:52:14.840
<v Neil Drumming>No, no, when you were in school.
<v Neil Drumming>不，不，当你上学时。

00:52:14.840 --> 00:52:16.520
<v Neil's Dad>[LAUGHS] Worked.
<v Neil's Dad>[笑声] 工作。

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:19.870
<v Neil's Dad>[LAUGHTER]
<v Neil's Dad>[笑声]

00:52:19.870 --> 00:52:22.410
<v Neil's Dad>That's what I tried to tell you.
<v Neil's Dad>这就是我试图告诉你的。

00:52:22.410 --> 00:52:29.490
<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHS]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑声]

00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:39.540
<v Ira Glass>We talked for over an hour. It was one of the longest conversations that I can remember us ever having. Every now and then, I'd try to steer him back to the question I wanted him to answer.
<v Ira Glass>我们聊了一个多小时。这是我记忆中我们进行过最长的谈话之一。偶尔，我会试图引导他回到我想让他回答的问题上。

00:52:39.540 --> 00:52:49.260
<v Neil Drumming>So I know I asked you this a bunch of times. I keep asking you the same question. You can tell me to stop asking you if you want, but did you have fun yourself?
<v Neil Drumming>所以我知道我问过你很多次了。我一直问你同一个问题。如果你想的话，你可以叫我停止问你，但你觉得开心吗？

00:52:49.260 --> 00:53:12.270
<v Neil's Dad>Yeah. See, I don't regret anything because it looked to me like I was doing what I was supposed to do. And to see you kids happy was to be happy too. And you guys could always come in and do whatever it is, and go back out to the pool or whatever.
<v Neil's Dad>是的。你看，我不后悔任何事情，因为它看起来我是在做我应该做的事情。看到你们孩子们快乐就是快乐。而且你们总是可以进来做任何事，然后回到游泳池或什么的。

00:53:12.270 --> 00:53:13.060
<v Neil Drumming>Mhm.
<v Neil Drumming>嗯哼。

00:53:13.060 --> 00:53:19.650
<v Neil's Dad>I remember you guys playing out there and hanging around the bushes and stuff.
<v Neil's Dad>我记得你们在那里玩，在灌木丛周围转悠之类的。

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:21.090
<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHS]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑声]

00:53:21.090 --> 00:53:23.160
<v Neil's Dad>I thought it was great.
<v Neil's Dad>我觉得这很棒。

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:53.330
<v Neil Drumming>That's a kind of enjoyment I hadn't considered. I live more selfishly. Also, his answer was hard to take in, to reconcile with the distance I felt between us at the time, back when he would retire to the couch to watch TV or we went off to play on our own. Maybe he was watching me play in the bushes and getting a kick out of it, but I didn't know that. Still, I was happy at the villa, and my dad says he was too. I'm glad I know that.
<v Neil Drumming>那是我没考虑过的一种享受。我活得更自私。而且，他的回答很难接受，很难与我当时感到的我们之间的距离相协调，那时他会退到沙发上看电视，或者我们自己去玩。也许他看着我在灌木丛里玩，并从中得到了乐趣，但我不知道。不过，在别墅我很快乐，我爸爸说他也很快乐。我很高兴知道这一点。

00:53:53.330 --> 00:54:11.890
<v Neil Drumming>All right. So I have been talking to you for an hour. I should probably let you go. But hey, is it OK if I call back this week and just talk? I want to hear more stuff since I didn't seem interested when I was a kid. I didn't realize that was why you didn't tell us stuff. So now I'll just ask. Is that OK if I could--
<v Neil Drumming>好了。我跟你聊了一个小时了。我可能应该让你去休息了。但是嘿，如果我这周再打电话回来只是聊聊天可以吗？我想听听更多的事情，因为当我还是个孩子时我似乎并不感兴趣。我没意识到那就是你没告诉我们事情的原因。所以现在我只是会问。如果我可以——这样可以吗？

00:54:11.890 --> 00:54:22.980
<v Neil's Dad>The only thing I'd do is get up, and sometimes I'm outside just walking around. Sometimes I sit down. Sometimes I go ride the bike. And I do this just to keep busy.
<v Neil's Dad>我唯一会做的是起床，有时我在外面只是到处走走。有时我坐下来。有时我去骑自行车。我这样做只是为了保持忙碌。

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:23.900
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。

00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:25.530
<v Neil's Dad>You can call me anytime.
<v Neil's Dad>你可以随时给我打电话。

00:54:25.530 --> 00:54:27.220
<v Neil Drumming>All right. I'm going to go back to work.
<v Neil Drumming>好的。我要回去工作了。

00:54:27.220 --> 00:54:27.670
<v Neil's Dad>OK.
<v Neil's Dad>好的。

00:54:27.670 --> 00:54:28.190
<v Neil Drumming>Bye, Dad.
<v Neil Drumming>再见，爸爸。

00:54:28.190 --> 00:54:36.800
<v Neil's Dad>Bye.
<v Neil's Dad>再见。

00:54:36.800 --> 00:54:50.630
<v Ira Glass>Neil Drumming. He says his dad, who's 87, is emphatically not freaked out over COVID-19.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔·德拉明。他说他87岁的爸爸，明确地没有被新冠病毒吓坏。

00:54:50.630 --> 00:54:53.430
<v Ira Glass>Act Five, "Let's Talk Radio."
<v Ira Glass>第五幕，“谈谈收音机”。

00:54:53.430 --> 00:55:12.960
<v Man>We'd like to ask you this question, my friend. Do personal problems and worries have you down? Are you disturbed by business problems, marriage problems, or emotional problems? See Mrs. K, reader and advisor. Mrs. K, formerly of Europe, gives you a reading and answers all your questions for just $1.00. And you'll feel much better.
<v Man>我的朋友，我们想问你这个问题。个人问题和烦恼让你消沉了吗？你被商业问题、婚姻问题或情感问题困扰了吗？去看看K太太，读者和顾问。曾经在欧洲的K太太给你进行一次解读，只花1美元就回答你所有的问题。你会感觉好多了。

00:55:12.960 --> 00:55:30.790
<v Ira Glass>OK. So this is my father in 1956, three years before I was born. He's 23 years old in this recording. And it's amazing, to me, anyway, hearing him so young, doing something I know so well, trying to sound like you're just talking, totally relaxed, when, in fact, you're reading.
<v Ira Glass>好了。所以这是我爸爸在1956年，在我出生前三年。他在录音中23岁。这很神奇，对我来说，听到他如此年轻，做着我如此熟悉的事情，试图听起来像你只是在说话，完全放松，而事实上，你是在读稿。

00:55:30.790 --> 00:55:43.470
<v Ira's Dad>And coming up right now, news about a wonderful appliance from Norman R. Mitchell just for the homemakers, as we said before. We're talking about an electric dryer. And, my friend--
<v Ira's Dad>现在接下来是，关于来自诺曼·R·米切尔的一款很棒的电器的消息，专门针对家庭主妇，正如我们之前所说。我们谈论的是一台电动干衣机。而且，我的朋友——

00:55:43.470 --> 00:56:02.740
<v Ira Glass>My father started in radio when he was 19, the same age I was when I started. He began at the college station at the University of Maryland, then after graduation he got a job spinning records at a commercial station in Baltimore. He loved it. He continued doing this on Sunday mornings while he was in the Army, stationed in Virginia. That's where this recording is from.
<v Ira Glass>我父亲19岁时就开始从事广播，和我刚开始时一样的年龄。他始于马里兰大学的校园电台，毕业后他在巴尔的摩的一家商业电台找到了一份播放唱片的工作。他很喜欢。当他在弗吉尼亚州驻扎服兵役时，他继续在周日早上做这件事。这段录音就是那时制作的。

00:56:02.740 --> 00:56:35.840
<v Ira Glass>And at some point my mom got pregnant a second time with me, and he decided to quit radio. It just didn't pay enough. The most he ever made at a radio job was $90 a week, which wasn't much money even then. And at that point he became the person that I knew him as, a certified public accountant. Years ago, I called him for a story for our show to ask about that decision to leave radio, and it was interesting. There was no sentimentality at all, like nothing.
<v Ira Glass>在某个时候，我妈妈第二次怀孕了，有了我，他决定退出广播。它付出的报酬不够。他在广播工作中赚的最多的就是每周90美元，即使在当时那也不是很多钱。从那时起，他成了我所认识的那个人，一名注册会计师。多年前，我为了节目的一个故事打电话给他询问离开广播的那个决定，这很有趣。没有任何多愁善感，就像什么都没有。

00:56:35.840 --> 00:57:00.590
<v Ira's Dad>By that time, I had realized that radio was not for me. What happened would be a new program director would come in, and if you weren't the apple of that guy's eye then you were out of a job. You got to go start looking for a job again. Even though that never happened to me, I could see it happening to other people. And I wanted to be in control of my own destiny, and I decided that it wasn't going to work out. Radio was not going to work out.
<v Ira's Dad>到那时，我已经意识到广播不适合我。发生的情况通常是新节目总监进来了，如果你不是那个家伙的“眼中钉”，那么你就丢掉工作了。你得去重新寻找工作。即使这种情况从来没发生在我身上，我也能看到它发生在别人身上。而且我想掌握自己的命运，我决定它不会成功的。广播不会成功的。

00:57:00.590 --> 00:57:01.700
<v Ira Glass>And that was 1959?
<v Ira Glass>那是1959年？

00:57:01.700 --> 00:57:02.370
<v Ira's Dad>Yeah.
<v Ira's Dad>是的。

00:57:02.370 --> 00:57:03.320
<v Ira Glass>The year I was born.
<v Ira Glass>我出生的那一年。

00:57:03.320 --> 00:57:04.640
<v Ira's Dad>Right.
<v Ira's Dad>没错。

00:57:04.640 --> 00:57:06.310
<v Ira Glass>Are those two things related?
<v Ira Glass>这两件事有关联吗？

00:57:06.310 --> 00:57:09.440
<v Ira's Dad>Not at all. [LAUGHS]
<v Ira's Dad>一点也没有。[笑声]

00:57:09.440 --> 00:57:10.780
<v Ira Glass>It sounds like they are.
<v Ira Glass>听起来好像是。

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:13.000
<v Ira's Dad>No, they're not. No, they're not.
<v Ira's Dad>不，它们没有。不，它们没有。

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:34.570
<v Ira Glass>I don't know if he wants to take me out of the equation, so I don't feel bad for him quitting radio, but I don't believe him. I do believe that, even in his private moments, today he doesn't regret the decision. His hearing is so bad these days, even with hearing aids, I doubt that he'll hear this story on the radio or online.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道他是否想把我从方程中去掉，所以我不会为他退出广播感到难过，但我并不相信他。我相信，即使在他私人的时刻，今天他也不后悔这个决定。他的听力这些天很差，即使有助听器，我怀疑他是否会在广播或网上听到这个故事。

00:57:34.570 --> 00:57:51.260
<v Ira Glass>I talked to him a couple times this week. He turns 87 on Monday, so part of the population in greatest danger right now. He said he and my stepmom aren't going out. They just figured out how to get groceries delivered. And I'm worried for them.
<v Ira Glass>本周我跟他谈了几次。他周一就87岁了，所以是现在处在最大危险中的那部分人口。他说他和我的继母不出门。他们刚搞清楚如何把杂货送到家。我为他们担心。

00:57:51.260 --> 00:58:19.570
<v Ira's Dad>Well, all the stars are on record, and all the records star on the Sunday morning carousel. Coming up for you right now, our featured top tune of the day, number one in the record stores we visited this week in Baltimore.
<v Ira's Dad>嗯，所有的明星都在录音上，所有的唱片都在周日上午的旋转木马上。接下来是为您准备的，我们今日精选的顶级曲目，在我们这周在巴尔的摩访问的唱片店中排名第一。

00:58:19.570 --> 00:58:38.750
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Diane Wu. Our staff includes Emanuele Berry, Susan Burton, Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Noor Gill, Lina Misitzis, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Alissa Shipp, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Julie Whitaker. Our executive editor is David Kestenbaum. Special thanks today to Tony [? Lynn, ?] John [? Elgin, ?] Danielle Elliott, and Chris Crawford.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由黛安·吴制作。我们的员工包括埃曼纽尔·贝里、苏珊·伯顿、佐伊·蔡斯、达娜·奇维斯、肖恩·科尔、努尔·吉尔、莉娜·米西齐斯、斯托·尼尔森、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、纳迪亚·雷曼、艾丽莎·希普、克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉、马特·蒂尔尼和朱莉·惠特克。我们的执行编辑是戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆。今天特别感谢托尼·林恩、约翰·埃尔金、丹妮尔·埃利奥特和克里斯·克劳福德。

00:58:38.750 --> 00:58:55.340
<v Ira Glass>Our website, ThisAmericanLife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. Trying to be civic-minded and conserve on toilet paper, he tried out a bidet for the first time this week.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，ThisAmericanLife.org。这份《美国生活》由PRX，即公共广播交流中心，传送到公共广播电台。像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生。为了尽到公民责任并节省卫生纸，他这周第一次尝试了坐浴盆。

00:58:55.340 --> 00:58:59.270
<v Rosie>I was like, oh, well, this isn't really exactly what I wanted.
<v Rosie>我就说，哦，好吧，这并不完全是我想要的。

00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:04.270
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周回来带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
